So: yes, I think the metadata-buffer coherency layer needs to change, not get patched again. Let me get your call on the direction.
You chose the hard cache-barrier re-architecture. That's the right call — and the key safety insight that makes it work is **evict AFTER write, at yield**: once a buffer is drained (written durable), staling it loses nothing, and cold-read on acquire gets the durable image. That even makes it safe for AGI (the sess103 trap was evicting a *this-node-ahead, not-yet-written* buffer at read time — different point).

